DESCRIPTION:https://events.stanford.edu/event/overt-listening-2026-a-screen
 ing-and-conversation-with-tiffany-sia\n\nStanford Cinematheque\, a graduat
 e student-run film collective based in the Department of Art & Art History
 \, presents a screening of Overt Listening\, followed by a discussion with
  filmmaker Tiffany Sia.\n\nAbout the Film\nKinmen is a cluster of Taiwanes
 e islands several kilometers from the Chinese coastline. It is also a nati
 onal park. Overt Listening (2026) is Tiffany Sia's debut feature-length fi
 lm\, an essay film without voiceover that moves through Kinmen's pristine 
 shorelines\, roadside bunker attractions\, underground military tunnels\, 
 and traffic circles centering statues of Chiang Kai-shek and Sun Yat-sen\,
  myriad sites preserved by Taiwan's national park services that attempt to
  calcify the territory's Cold War history into spectacle and memorial. In 
 place of spoken language\, the film works through the intertitle—owing t
 o early cinema's grammar of silent film—as an interstitial voice: at onc
 e historical record\, suppressed testimony\, and what the landscape doesn'
 t show. While 1989 through 1991 are commonly regarded as the years global 
 Cold War tensions eased\, Overt Listening works to discern a more difficul
 t-to-read\, ongoing paradigm. The film relies on various aural presences
 —ranging from silence\, signal\, noise\, and song—where military activ
 ity is distantly heard but not seen\, and silence is loaded\, itself unpea
 ceful.\n\nAbout the Filmmaker\nTiffany Sia (b. 1988) is an artist\, filmma
 ker\, and writer. Sia's films have screened at the New York Film Festival\
 , Toronto International 
